    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d788">

                <pb facs="1070" n="1056" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1056"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.50/6–3044</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2729</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-06-30">June 30,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received July 15.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: China’s Postwar Reconstruction Plans.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s airgram A–128, May 23, 1944,<anchor n="28" xml:id="d788-f1"/> on
                    the above subject, and to enclose a copy of a memorandum of conversation of June
                    28, 1944 between an officer of the Embassy and Mr. K. P. Chen, Chairman of the
                    Economic Planning <pb facs="1071" n="1057" xml:id="pg1057"/> Commission, who
                    stated that the report issued by the Chinese News Service was “altogether
                    premature” and that the final report of his Commission would probably not be
                    completed until about the end of September 1944.</p>
                <p>The Embassy will expect to report additional information, when available, in
                    regard to the progress made by the Chinese authorities in formulating postwar
                    reconstruction plans. Several Chinese officials and business men, assumably well
                    informed, have stated to officers of the Embassy that no agreement has yet been
                    reached regarding any reconstruction plans. These statements appear to be
                    authentic, judging by the remarks attributed to Mr. Chen in the enclosed
                    memorandum.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d788-a1.xml" xpointer="d788-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
